🧩 Architecture Components:
Frontend & API Layer
React UI: User interface for monitoring and managing the platform.

Node.js API: Middleware that bridges frontend interactions with backend orchestrations.

Core Orchestration Layer
LangGraph Orchestrator:

Acts as the central brain coordinating all agent activities.

Receives input from UI/API and GitLab repo.

Dispatches tasks to agents based on context or alerts.

ML Monitoring Agents
DriftDetectorAg:

Monitors deployed ML models for data, concept, or performance drift.

Sends alerts or insights to the orchestrator.

PerformanceAgent:

Continuously checks model latency, accuracy, and resource usage.

Sends performance anomalies to the GitFixerAgent.

Remediation & Alert Agents
GitFixerAgent:

Analyzes issues raised by agents (e.g., performance drops).

Proposes or pushes code/config fixes back to the GitLab Repo.

Triggers retraining/deployment via Azure ML.

NotifierAgent:

Sends alerts or updates (e.g., Slack, email, dashboard) to stakeholders about issues or fixes.

CI/CD + MLOps
GitLab Repo:

Stores ML code, configs, pipelines.

Serves as a trigger point for changes detected by agents.

Integrated with Azure ML for training/deployment automation.

Azure ML:

Handles model training, evaluation, and deployment.

🔁 Workflow Summary:
User or automation triggers start in the React UI.

Input goes to LangGraph Orchestrator via Node.js API.

Orchestrator dispatches tasks to:

DriftDetectorAg for drift monitoring.

PerformanceAgent for health/performance.

On issue detection, GitFixerAgent attempts auto-remediation:

Updates GitLab repo.

Triggers Azure ML to retrain/redeploy.

NotifierAgent informs stakeholders about the incident & resolution.

✅ What It Enables:
Proactive monitoring of ML models.

Auto-remediation for model degradation or drift.

CI/CD integration for seamless fix-deploy cycles.

AI agents working in sync via LangGraph.
